        "self_reported": {
          "assumptions": [
            "The brief's 'hand line names every group with survivors' is read as every group not known to be gone (survivors, no /proc, twine kill's own), the runtime's first then record order \u2014 the same set 5b's table named for one group.",
            "The one re-read also treats a runtime that re-registered under a new pgid as one more group to kill; the brief names only groups registered since.",
            "`twine status --state-dir ' '` (an empty flag) is reported as a null state_dir with spend's bad-argument text as the reason, not a usage error: status is read-only and ok is untouched.",
            "Extra keys on the running record or on a group entry are ignored on reading, as 5b's reader ignored them; 'exactly six keys' binds what twine writes and which keys the reader requires."
          ],
          "judgment_calls": [
            "register_group runs its read-append-replace under an exclusive flock on the running/ directory (no lock file beside the record): the independent review showed 3 of 20 concurrent registrations landing without it, and a lost entry is exactly a kill that reports ok while a tool runs on.",
            "When the runtime's recorded group is twine kill's own, the step stops there and the groups after it are not signalled either \u2014 the contract's 'the step stops' kept, with the brief's reason (the runtime that could start more is not stopped).",
            "`locate` is no longer called by the kill verb; bale.locate_executable runs in its place so the report carries the bale object on every path and nothing joins refusals. carry_bale.locate is unchanged for the carry verbs.",
            "A record that becomes malformed on the re-read stops the process step with operator_line null and the record left in place; one that is gone on the re-read is nothing more to do (record_cleared false, since someone else cleared it).",
            "A pgid the record names twice (or that equals the runtime's) is signalled once and reported once in process.groups.",
            "The unreadable-state-directory refusal (no search permission on a parent) found by the review is fixed in both status and kill in the same stroke, with the kill's permission case tested only when the suite runs unprivileged (root bypasses mode bits).",
            "The six session assertions in validation.sh that start real process groups write their pids to files and poll /proc for death; nothing in it checks once."
          ],
